WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A 4.1 billion-year-old Martian meteorite offers the oldest direct sample of the red planet's crust and mantle. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 238. After that rock, asteroid fragments that survive white-dashed dwarf tides, super typhoon champion through Sentinel-3, and a plant disease experiment on the International Space Station. Teghasa 001 matches mineralogy and chemistry at 13 Highland sites in Mars' southern hemisphere, according to the National Aeronautics

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.820
in Space Administration, or NASA.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.200
A global map of those highlands was built from Mars' reconnaissance orbiter data that

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.420
picked out similar iron titanium oxides, pyroxene and shocked plagioclase.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:49.120
Oxygen isotope values and manganese rich domains inside the stone record early fluid activity

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:49.620
on Mars.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.940
The sample is a single unadulterated rock type rather than a breccia, so its age and alteration

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.440
history can be read directly.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.080
Zircon crystals dated by uranium lead methods confirmed that age.

00:01:02.921 --> 00:01:07.521
The stone was recovered from the Sahara, and it records the planet's first crust before

00:01:07.521 --> 00:01:08.741
Mars cooled and dried.

00:01:09.361 --> 00:01:13.961
Researchers at the University of New Mexico recognized the meteorites' unusual features

00:01:13.961 --> 00:01:18.141
and coordinated the multi-institution analysis that dated those zircons.

00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:24.886
New modeling carried by Fizz.org finds that rubble-pile asteroids near white dwarfs keep internal

00:01:24.886 --> 00:01:27.346
strength from Vanderwals forces between grains.

00:01:27.346 --> 00:01:31.946
mains. Most of the mass remains locked in chunks between a tenth of a kilometer and a

00:01:31.946 --> 00:01:37.446
full kilometer, instead of becoming dust right away. Pointing Robertson drag cannot sweep

00:01:37.446 --> 00:01:42.966
away objects that size on a short time scale. Extra collisions or spin-up events still have

00:01:42.966 --> 00:01:47.246
to grind the pieces down before dust can feed the disks astronomers actually see.

00:01:48.206 --> 00:01:53.206
The work appears in the astrophysical journal letters. After the breakup, the pieces stay on

00:01:53.206 --> 00:01:57.886
nearly identical paths, so the debris stays more bunched than older models had predicted.

00:01:58.466 --> 00:02:03.066
White dwarfs pack roughly the mass of the Sun into a sphere the size of Earth, and the

00:02:03.066 --> 00:02:08.046
gravitational gradient across that compact remnant is steep enough to tear apart any asteroid

00:02:08.046 --> 00:02:13.306
that drifts inside the Roche limit. Take a body as long as a city block and send it toward

00:02:13.306 --> 00:02:18.526
one of those remnants. Gravity on the near face exceeds the far face by a factor of thousands.

00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:23.806
Models that once treated rubble piles like loose sand now give real asteroids a minimum

00:02:23.806 --> 00:02:28.046
tensile strength roughly 10 to 100 times that of freshly fallen snow.

00:02:28.706 --> 00:02:33.346
Jordan Steckloff at the Planetary Science Institute shows that a kilometer-sized barrier

00:02:33.346 --> 00:02:36.946
then limits how far white dwarf tides can break an asteroid apart.

00:02:37.686 --> 00:02:42.706
White dwarf gravity runs the same disruption physics at far higher accelerations than anything

00:02:42.706 --> 00:02:43.926
in our own solar system.

00:02:43.926 --> 00:02:50.526
Comet Shoemaker-Livi 9 did a slower version of this when it passed Jupiter in 1992, splitting

00:02:50.526 --> 00:02:53.066
into large fragments that later struck the planet.

00:02:53.626 --> 00:02:58.626
What remains unknown is how often the surviving pieces collide or get torn apart a second

00:02:58.626 --> 00:03:03.666
time, and whether that later debris can explain the infrared excesses around many white

00:03:03.666 --> 00:03:04.146
dwarfs.

00:03:04.646 --> 00:03:09.366
Until someone catches a fragment in the act of breaking further, the path from kilometer

00:03:09.366 --> 00:03:12.266
rock to observable dust stays an open calculation.

00:03:12.266 --> 00:03:19.687
Capernicus Sentinel-3, flown by the European Space Agency, or ESA, captured super typhoon

00:03:19.687 --> 00:03:24.467
champion on October 21st, 2026, off Taiwan and the Philippines.

00:03:25.187 --> 00:03:29.907
The false color frame assigns green, white, and light gray to the coldest cloud tops

00:03:29.907 --> 00:03:31.627
at minus 70 degrees Celsius.

00:03:32.327 --> 00:03:38.127
Purple marks clouds between minus 30 and minus 15 degrees Celsius, while orange shows ocean

00:03:38.127 --> 00:03:40.767
and land surfaces near 25 degrees Celsius.

00:03:41.447 --> 00:03:45.347
Maximum sustained winds reach 296 kilometers per hour.

00:03:45.907 --> 00:03:47.447
The strongest recorded this year.

00:03:48.187 --> 00:03:51.967
Waves near the center hit 10 meters and drove flooding across eastern Taiwan.

00:03:52.747 --> 00:03:58.787
The sea and land surface temperature radiometer supplied the thermal infrared radiance used for the brightness temperature map.

00:03:59.347 --> 00:04:03.907
Cold cloud tops are seen moving over warm ocean water that feeds the storm.

00:04:04.507 --> 00:04:10.047
And those brightness temperatures are not the same as actual temperatures even as the color scale lays out the structure.

00:04:10.047 --> 00:04:16.832
University of Florida researchers, in work carried by Fizz.org, are testing how microgravity

00:04:16.832 --> 00:04:21.392
and radiation affect plant pathogens on the International Space Station, or ISS.

00:04:22.272 --> 00:04:27.172
The run is meant to protect future crops grown aboard spacecraft or on planetary surfaces.

00:04:27.772 --> 00:04:32.552
Long duration missions will rely on fresh food production, so disease resistance is

00:04:32.552 --> 00:04:33.732
a practical requirement.

00:04:34.352 --> 00:04:38.872
The experiment is gathering that data on station before crews travel farther from Earth.

00:04:38.872 --> 00:04:44.291
High pressure laboratory work has compressed magnesium and silicon compounds to mantle

00:04:44.291 --> 00:04:48.911
conditions of gas giants and larger rocky worlds, according to Universe Today.

00:04:49.611 --> 00:04:53.831
Crystal structures shift with pressure and temperature in ways not seen inside Earth.

00:04:54.611 --> 00:04:58.691
Magnesium oxide forms under those extreme pressures in planetary interiors.

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:03.311
The team paired laboratory compression with computer simulations to map the differences.

00:05:03.311 --> 00:05:08.351
Those maps are being used to model how super-earth and gas giant cores evolve.

00:05:09.019 --> 00:05:13.939
Cape Canaveral has hosted more orbital attempts this year than in any previous calendar year,

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:15.379
according to the Future Party.

00:05:15.959 --> 00:05:20.579
Multiple operators are using the range at the same time for crude, cargo, and national

00:05:20.579 --> 00:05:21.619
security missions.

00:05:22.099 --> 00:05:26.079
Demand is rising across commercial, government, and international partners.

00:05:26.519 --> 00:05:29.719
The cadence has climbed as new vehicles and pads come online.

00:05:29.719 --> 00:05:34.314
Starling shells now sit within 10 kilometers of Hubble's altitude.

00:05:34.914 --> 00:05:38.934
Public tracking data show roughly 6,000 of those satellites at average heights

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:42.774
between 463 kilometers and 483 kilometers.

00:05:43.394 --> 00:05:49.894
Hubble currently averages 469.5 kilometers and is still descending about 1 kilometer per month.

00:05:50.414 --> 00:05:55.234
Different orbital inclinations mean the shells only cross the telescope's path at certain points.

00:05:55.234 --> 00:06:01.254
Under a 2021 agreement, Starlink satellites maneuver to avoid Hubble, which has no thrusters

00:06:01.254 --> 00:06:01.914
of its own.

00:06:02.924 --> 00:06:07.384
Norway is receiving three ESA contracts for jam-resistant satellite navigation.

00:06:07.944 --> 00:06:14.344
The work will build a library of global navigation satellite system, or GNSS, interference recordings,

00:06:14.944 --> 00:06:19.984
field maritime sensors that keep working under jamming, and test drone navigation in Arctic

00:06:19.984 --> 00:06:20.904
denial conditions.

00:06:20.904 --> 00:06:25.304
data collection starts with Norway's annual jammer test exercise.

00:06:25.644 --> 00:06:29.804
The contract sits under ESA's navigation innovation and support program.

00:06:30.424 --> 00:06:32.564
Norway hosts key Galileo infrastructure

00:06:32.564 --> 00:06:36.384
and has already studied the economic impact of a seven-day positioning,

00:06:36.944 --> 00:06:39.664
navigation and timing, or PNT outage.

00:06:40.184 --> 00:06:43.604
The signal data library is meant to give industry realistic jamming

00:06:43.604 --> 00:06:45.304
and spoofing scenarios for testing.

00:06:46.466 --> 00:06:51.486
Dragonfly, a rotorcraft bound for Titan, is collecting names from the public to fly aboard

00:06:51.486 --> 00:06:57.206
the spacecraft. NASA says the mission launches in 2028 to explore Titan's dunes, lakes,

00:06:57.486 --> 00:07:02.546
and rivers. Names submitted through the agency's site will travel on the vehicle. The rotor

00:07:02.546 --> 00:07:08.226
craft will study prebiotic chemistry and habitability on that moon. The campaign continues a line

00:07:08.226 --> 00:07:13.850
of public name collection efforts used on earlier missions. Voyager 1 has entered a power

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:19.090
conservation mode while engineers diagnose a recent signal interruption. The pause itself

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:23.910
was resolved after that power adjustment. The spacecraft is still returning data from

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:30.864
interstellar space despite its age and distance. Caltech has received $27.5 million in gifts

00:07:30.864 --> 00:07:37.044
earmarked for space exploration initiatives, according to Pasadena now. The donations coincide

00:07:37.044 --> 00:07:41.684
with the arrival of the university's new president. The money supports research and education

00:07:41.684 --> 00:07:45.284
at the institution that operates NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

00:07:46.161 --> 00:07:50.521
The cat's head nebula in Karina lies 7,500 light years away

00:07:50.871 --> 00:07:53.041
and resembles a cat viewed from behind.

00:07:53.721 --> 00:07:57.101
Hubble's visible light frame, in coverage carried by Fizz.org,

00:07:57.581 --> 00:08:00.401
shows an opaque dust column with a red glowing eye.

00:08:01.141 --> 00:08:04.721
Webb's infrared data reveal embedded stars and background galaxies.

00:08:05.441 --> 00:08:09.881
Ultraviolet radiation and winds from nearby massive stars likely carved the pillar.

00:08:09.881 --> 00:08:14.601
Together, the two views trace star formation inside the larger Karina Nebula.

00:08:15.651 --> 00:08:20.771
In brief, Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutrick performed his first spacewalk

00:08:20.771 --> 00:08:24.371
as part of a day of Canadian station work, according to CBC.

00:08:25.211 --> 00:08:30.831
Engineers at Ames in a report from NBC Bay Area are refining landing trajectories and hazard

00:08:30.831 --> 00:08:35.951
avoidance procedures on a high-fidelity lunar simulator for upcoming crude, Artemis landings.

00:08:35.951 --> 00:08:41.831
Before we go, Dragonfly's 2028 departure for Titan is the dated flight on the books,

00:08:42.251 --> 00:08:45.311
and the name sealed on that rotorcraft will leave Earth with it.

